{
  "_comment": [
    "Shared ground truth for the two copies of the tint maths:",
    "accessible_ide/utils/colour.py (server, first paint and save) and",
    "static/js/tint.js (browser, live theme change).",
    "Both test suites read this file, so a disagreement between them fails a",
    "test instead of showing the reader a colour that is subtly wrong.",
    "",
    "Each case is a chosen tint against one theme's panel colour, with that",
    "theme's own three text colours. The expected value is that tint faded",
    "toward the panel, and only as far as it takes for all three of those",
    "text colours to stay readable on it. A tint that already reads comes",
    "back untouched.",
    "",
    "Cases marked \"tie\" land on a rounding boundary on the step that decides",
    "the answer. Python rounds ties to even, JavaScript rounds them up, so",
    "these are the cases that catch one copy drifting to the other's rounding",
    "rule. Every other case gives the same colour either way and cannot catch",
    "that.",
    "Themes are named so a failure says which one broke.",
    "",
    "Cases marked \"conversion\" cover the float and clamping paths in",
    "parse_hex and to_hex rather than the bisection: the differential sweep",
    "only ever passed hex strings, so it never reached them. Python's parse_hex",
    "truncated with int() and did not clamp, while parseHex rounded and",
    "clamped, so a float triple came out a channel apart and the browser built",
    "colours containing 256."
  ],
